S2[22] = {
1: "Idea: complex data with real coefficients run the filter twice, once on I and once on Q, at the input rate. 2 × 48 × 30.72 × 10⁶ = 2.95 × 10⁹ real multiplications per second. Check: Worked Example 22.1 had 2.56 × 10⁹ for 32 taps at 40 MS/s, and 48 × 30.72/(32 × 40) = 1.15 times that is 2.95 × 10⁹. The usual mistake is to count one rail only and come out a factor of two low.",
2: "Idea: each hard multiplier performs one multiplication per clock, so divide the rate by the clock. 2.95 × 10⁹/3.6864 × 10⁸ = 8.0, so 8 multipliers. Check: 8 × 368.64 MHz = 2.95 × 10⁹ per second exactly, since 30.72 × 96/368.64 = 8. The usual mistake is to give each tap its own multiplier, 96 in all, when the clock is twelve times the sample rate and lets each multiplier serve twelve taps.",
3: "Idea: a pre-adder sums the two samples that share a symmetric coefficient, so a 48-tap filter needs 24 multiplications per output per rail. The count halves to 4 multipliers. Check: 2 × 24 × 30.72 × 10⁶ = 1.47 × 10⁹ per second, and 1.47 × 10⁹/3.6864 × 10⁸ = 4.0. The usual mistake is to assume the saving applies to the adders too; the pre-adder adds an addition per pair.",
4: "Idea: each micro-rotation lengthens the vector by √(1 + 2^−2i), and the product converges. K = 1.6468, removed by one multiplication by 1/K = 0.6073 or folded into a later gain stage. Check: the first two factors alone are √2 × √1.25 = 1.581, already within 4 % of the limit. The usual mistake is to forget the gain and find every NCO output 4.3 dB too large.",
5: "Idea: the ideal 6.02B + 1.76 dB assumes a sine with 3 dB PAPR; a peakier signal loses the excess. 6.02B + 1.76 − (10 − 3) ≥ 35 gives B ≥ (35 − 1.76 + 7)/6.02 = 6.68, so 7 bits. Check: 7 bits give 42.1 + 1.76 − 7 = 36.9 dB, above 35; 6 bits give 30.9 dB, below. The usual mistake is to ignore the PAPR and choose 6 bits.",
6: "Idea: the ASIC's fixed cost is recovered by its lower price per unit. 1.5 × 10⁶/(35 − 5) = 50 000 units. Check: at 50 000 units the FPGA costs 1.75 million dollars and the ASIC 1.5 + 0.25 = 1.75 million, equal. The usual mistake is to divide the NRE by the ASIC's unit cost instead of by the difference in unit costs.",
7: "Idea: raw video rate is pixels × bytes per pixel × frames per second. 640 × 480 × 2 × 30 = 18.4 MB/s, 147 Mbit/s. Check: the 1600 × 1200 frame of Worked Example 22.6 has 6.25 times the pixels at half the frame rate, 3.1 times the rate, and 147 × 3.1 = 461 Mbit/s. The usual mistake is to forget the factor of 8 between bytes and bits.",
8: "Idea: power is operations per second times energy per operation. 10⁹ × 3 pJ = 3 mW; 10⁹ × 70 pJ = 70 mW. Check: a picojoule per operation at a gigaoperation per second is a milliwatt. The usual mistake is to compare only the arithmetic and forget that the processor also spends energy on every load and store, which can double its figure.",
9: "Idea: every path between two registers must settle within one clock period. A long adder tree has many adders in series, and its delay exceeds the period at high clock rates; registers between levels cut it into short stages, so the clock can run fast at the cost of a few cycles of latency. Check: Worked Example 22.3's CORDIC is pipelined the same way, one iteration per clock and 16 cycles of latency. The usual mistake is to lower the clock instead, which forces more parallel copies of the whole filter.",
10: "Idea: a full-scale tone puts all its energy into one bin, and each radix-2 stage can double the magnitude. log₂64 = 6 bits. Check: the DFT of a tone of amplitude A has a bin of magnitude NA = 64A, and 64 = 2⁶. The usual mistake is to allow only half a bit per stage, the growth for noise, and overflow on the first pure tone.",
}
